&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Huda Y. Zoghbi, MD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Distinguished Service Professor&amp;nbsp;at Baylor College of Medicine, Investigator, Howard Hughes Medical Institute and the Director of the Jan and Dan Duncan Neurological Research Institute (NRI) at Texas Children&amp;rsquo;s Hospital&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;quot;Rett syndrome and MECP2 disorders:&amp;nbsp;From the clinic to the bench and back&amp;quot;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted by Dr. Gerard Karsenty and Dr. Vincenzo Alessandro Gennarino&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;Department of Genetics and Development&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 10, 2025 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Ukrainian Studies Program and the Harriman Institute for a lecture by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Emina Muzaferija&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/tanya-domi/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tanya Domi&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In &amp;quot;Recovering Territory: The Thick Geopolitics of Demining in Postwar Bosnia-Herzegovina and Wartime Ukraine,&amp;quot; Dr. Emina Muzaferija conceptualizes demining as a geopolitical and territorial practice rather than a purely technical process. Through comparative analysis of Bosnia and Ukraine, the study develops the concept of &amp;ldquo;territorial recovery&amp;rdquo; to explain how demining reshapes governance, ecology, and sovereignty. Dr. Muzaferija shows that the geopolitical context&amp;mdash;whether anchored in peacebuilding or active conflict&amp;mdash;fundamentally transforms demining&amp;rsquo;s meaning and impact, revealing how practices of mine clearance both reverse and reproduce the spatial and political logics of war.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Program Overview&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Columbia University Irving Medical Center invites you to join the leading educators from Columbia University, addressing&amp;nbsp;Transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis (ATTR-CA), which is an underdiagnosed infiltrative cardiomyopathy resulting from the&amp;nbsp;deposition of&amp;nbsp;misfolded transthyretin proteins in the heart. Early detection and a multidisciplinary approach are essential for effective management.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Video Access&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All registrants will be sent access to view live lectures during the program and access to view the video recordings post-program.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Learning Objectives&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clinical presentation, diagnostic methods and risk stratification of patients with newly diagnosed ATTR-CA.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Management of patients with newly diagnosed systemic ATTR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Management of various organ involvement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Discuss and develop consensus in guidelines for managing relapsed and refractory cases, including the role of salvage therapies and experimental approaches.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
